"Charged Particles in Matter: teaching notes",
"Charged Particles in Matter",
"Medical Physics, charged particles in matter and proton therapy (upper level, calculus)",
"A charged particle loses energy mostly to electrons, at a rate set by its charge and speed and by the material's electrons per gram (Z/A) and mean excitation energy I: the Bethe formula. Below a few hundred MeV the {#}/β² factor dominates, so the slower the particle, the faster it loses energy.",
"The CSDA range is the integral R = ∫ dE/S. For protons in water over the therapy range it follows R = αE^p with p near {#}.",
"The Bragg peak comes from S rising as the protons slow. Range straggling (about {#} % of the range), the beam's energy spread and nuclear reactions give the real peak its finite height and width; the distal {#} % point sits at the CSDA range.",
"A slab moves the peak by its water equivalent thickness less its own thickness; the water equivalent thickness is its thickness times its stopping power relative to water.",
"Three views. <strong>Beam in water</strong>: a side view of a water tank with or without a slab in front (bone, lung, adipose, muscle, PMMA, graphite, aluminum, copper or lead), particle tracks fired one at a time and drawn colored by energy, and under it the depth dose curve, the model's broad beam curve with the dose the drawn tracks have put down. A detector chamber reads the dose (% of the peak), mean energy, stopping power and fluence at its depth. <strong>Stopping power</strong> and <strong>Range</strong>: mass stopping power and CSDA range against energy on log scales for any particle and material, with the NIST PSTAR ranges of protons in water and a power law fitted to them; electrons can be added to the stopping power graph (collision stopping only). Particles: protons, alpha particles and carbon {#} ions, {#} to {#} MeV per nucleon ({#} for carbon). Range straggling and nuclear reactions can be turned off, and the tracks stretched sideways ×{#} to see the scattering. Blind hides the model's ranges, peak and widths.",
