"<strong>\"The dose is largest where the beam enters, as for X rays.\"</strong> A proton beam's dose rises with depth, because each proton loses energy faster as it slows; most of it is put down near the end of range.",
"<strong>\"All protons of one energy stop at exactly the same depth.\"</strong> Energy loss is random; protons of one energy stop over a spread of about {#} % of the range.",
"<strong>\"Denser always means better at stopping.\"</strong> Per gram, materials with fewer electrons per nucleon and more tightly bound electrons (high Z) stop protons less well; per centimeter, density usually wins.",
"Electronic stopping: the Bethe formula with the full maximum energy transfer, S/ρ = K z²(Z/A)({#}/β²)[½ ln(2mₑc²β²γ²W_max/I²) − β²], K = {#} MeV cm²/mol, with no shell, Barkas, Bloch or density corrections, and the NIST PSTAR material data (density, I, composition; Z/A from the composition by weight). For protons in water (I = {#} eV) the CSDA ranges are within {#} mm of PSTAR from {#} to {#} MeV (curriculum/validation/test_vdstop.cjs). It is used down to {#} MeV per nucleon; a proton's last {#} MeV in water takes less than {#} mm. The core is shared with the other Medical Physics demos in demos/lib/vdstop.js.",
